// party always returns `409`. Didit creates a new hosted KYC session with the
// workflow the party was verified with (its latest published version) or, when
// that is no longer known, the workflow your Key People settings assign to the
// party's role, so its link lasts that KYC workflow's session expiration time;
// it emails the link to the party when an email is on file, clears
// `is_skipped`, and recomputes the Key People check and the business session
// status. It then sends a `data.updated`
// [webhook](https://docs.didit.me/integration/webhooks) for the business
// session with trigger `console_key_people_regenerate`; to exempt the party
// instead, use `PATCH /v3/session/{sessionId}/kyb/parties/{partyUuid}/`. A
// scoped API key needs `decisions: write`, and without `sessions: write` it
// receives `kyc_session_url` as `null`. Deleted sessions qualify even if the
// person had already started or finished verification. Imported sessions do not
// qualify through expiry; they must have been deleted.
//
//	POST /v3/session/{sessionId}/kyb/parties/{partyUuid}/regenerate/
